## ABSTRACT
The performance of solar cells is determined by three factors: the open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{{OC}}})$ , short-circuit current density $(J_{\mathsf{S C}})$ , and fill factor (FF). The $V_{\mathrm{{OC}}}$ and FF are determined by the material bandgap and the series/shunt resistance, respectively. However, $J_{\mathsf{S C}}$ is determined by the amount of incident light in addition to the bandgap of the material. In this study, a moth-eye pattern was formed on a glass surface via direct printing to increase the amount of incident light and thus increase $J_{\mathsf{S C}}$ . The moth-eye pattern is a typical antireflection pattern that reduces the reflection by gradually increasing the refractive index. A flat perovskite solar cell (F-PSC) and a moth-eye patterned perovskite solar cell (M-PSC) had $J_{\mathsf{S C}}$ values of 23.70 and $25.50\ \mathsf{m A}/\mathsf{c m}^{2}$ , respectively. The power-conversion efficiencies of the F-PSC and M-PSC were $19.81\%$ and $21.77\%$ , respectively.
## KEYWORDS
perovskite solar cells, direct printing, moth-eye pattern, hydrogen silsesquioxane
## Introduction
Nowadays, most people agree that ecofriendly energy harvesting is vital to the upcoming $4^{\mathrm{th}}$ industrial revolution. Solar cells are among the most important energy-harvesting devices for converting the most abundant energy source (sunlight) into electricity directly without pollution and greenhouse-gas emission [1]. Recently, solution-processed organic–inorganic hybrid perovskite solar cells (PSCs) have attracted attention owing to their high performance and the unique properties of hybrid perovskite light absorbers, such as their high absorption coefficient, small effective mass of carriers, high carrier mobility, long carrier diffusion length, low exciton binding energy, dominant point defects that generate only shallow trap levels, and appropriate energy bandgap for photovoltaic devices [2–9]. Furthermore, PSCs are attractive for commercialization owing to the low-cost thin-film fabrication method with facile solution processing, such as spin coating, spray coating, dip coating, and die or knife coating at a low processing temperature [10–15]. In recent years, the power-conversion efficiency (PCE) of PSCs has been significantly increased from $3.8\%$ to $25.2\%$ [16–21], but this value is still lower than that of Si-based solar cells.
One simple approach (among the several strategies used to enhance the PCE) is to improve the light absorption for increasing the short-circuit current density $(J_{\mathrm{SC}})$ ; to achieve this, a high-efficiency antireflection (AR) coating is required on the externally facing surfaces of the transparent films to reduce the surface reflection. One of the most commonly used methods for enhancing the light absorption is to apply nanopatterned
AR films, which contain flexible and stretchable polymer-based materials, on the AR side of the glass plate [22, 23]. Various AR surface coatings have been used, such as single-layer interface coatings [24, 25], multi-layer interface coatings [26], surface texturing [27], and moth-eye structure forming [22, 28]. Although numerous studies have achieved an increase in the light absorption using AR approaches on PSCs, few studies have reported applying moth-eye structure forming. However, the moth-eye structure has several merits as an AR coating: Bernhard determined that by structuring nanosized patterns on the surface, the effects of the moth-eye structure introduce a gradual change in the refractive index, and the reflection of incident light can thus be minimized over the whole spectral range and used to maximize the light-harvesting efficiency (LHE) [29]. In this respect, Kang et al. demonstrated the nanopatterned mesoporous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ (mpTiO2) layers were used as a conical shaped moth-eye structure to enhance the LHE of PCEs, and the moth-eye ${500-}\mathrm{nm}$ dorm structure of the $\mathrm{mpTiO}_{2}$ layer was fabricated to enhance the LHE and increase the $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ from 22.86 to 23.92 $\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ [28]. However, this method cannot be easily applied to planar-type PSCs, because the moth-eye pattern is placed on the electron-transport layer (ETL) inside the solar cell. The pattern of the ETL layer results in high surface roughness, which can cause difficulties in coating the perovskite uniformly.
We report the use of a moth-eye patterned hydrogen silsesquioxane (HSQ, $n\approx1.4$ ) structure on the outside of a glass substrate that can be successfully applied to improve the light absorption of perovskite. The merits of the moth-eye patterned HSQ structure layer on the outside of the glass substrate are as follows: 1) A plane surface can be formed on transparent conductive oxide (TCO) layers (such as indium tin oxide (ITO) and fluorine-doped tin oxide (FTO)), and therefore a compact and thin ETL can be easily induced from the flat surface on the TCO glass substrate. 2) Compared to polydimethylsiloxane (PDMS), HSQ structure is suitable for large area. In the case of small size PSC, it is not a problem to attach patterned PDMS without air bubbles, but it is difficult of large area. On the other hand, in this paper, since the HSQ pattern is formed on the glass via the direct printing, the reflection increase by the air bubbles does not occur. 3) In terms of material, HSQ is more suitable than PDMS. Because PDMS is a polymer, it is degraded by ultraviolet rays and moisture. In particular, since it is used in solar cells, it is difficult to avoid degradation. However, since HSQ is an inorganic compound, it does not cause degradation by sunlight. Using a moth-eye pattern with a $280\mathrm{-nm}$ -pitch structure formed via direct printing, we fabricated highperformance PSCs with enhanced absorption. When the period of a nanostructure is sufficiently smaller than the wavelength of light, the structure is recognized as a single medium from the viewpoint of light. In this respect, the repeated periods and forms of the moth-eye pattern are recognized as a medium in which the effective reflective index gradually changes, reducing the reflectance regardless of the wavelength. Direct printing, which was used to form the moth-eye pattern in this study, is suitable for energy fields where cost-effectiveness is important, owing to its simple, inexpensive, and large-scale process. Consequently, there was an increase of $\sim3.1\%$ in the photocurrent density. Therefore, the performance of the PCE can be improved from $19.99\%$ to $21.77\%$ by using the moth-eye pattern as an AR film.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Fabrication of moth-eye patterned FTO (M-FTO) on glass side
Figure 1 shows the fabrication process for the moth-eye patterned PSC (M-PSC). The polymer mold used in the direct-printing process is typically a PDMS mold. However, if the pattern is fine or the aspect ratio is high, a hard-PDMS (H-PDMS) mold is used, because the PDMS mold may not be fully detached. The solution for the H-PDMS comprised $3.4~\mathrm{g}$ of trimethylsiloxyterminatedvinylmethyl-siloxane-dimethylsiloxane (VDT-731, Gelest), $18\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of $\mathrm{Pt}$ catalyst (SIP6831.2, Gelest), $0.1\mathrm{\g}$ of 2,4,6,8- tetramethyltetravinylcyclotetra-siloxane (Aldrich), and $_{\textrm{1g}}$ of methylhydrosiloxanedimethyl-siloxane (HMS-301, Gelest) copolymers in $2~\mathrm{mL}$ of toluene (Aldrich) [30]. The mixture was spin-coated on the master stamp of the moth-eye pattern (Temicon GmbH) at $^{3,000}\mathrm{rpm}$ for $60~\mathsf{s}$ and cured in oven at
$80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^3\mathrm{h}$ . Then, PDMS was poured onto the H-PDMS to facilitate handling. A mixture of Sylgard 184A and 184B (10:1) was poured onto the H-PDMS, followed by curing in an oven at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize2h}$ . After the curing, the H-PDMS mold was detached from the master stamp, and HSQ was spin-coated onto the patterned H-PDMS at $3,000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $30~\mathsf{s}.$ The glass surface of the FTO was placed on the H-PDMS and pressed at 5 bar for $5~\mathrm{min}$ , and the moth-eye pattern was formed via direct printing.
## 2.2 Perovskite solar cell fabrication
An FTO-coated glass substrate (FTO glass, $7.09\Omega/\mathrm{sq})$ was used as the substrate for the devices. The substrates were rinsed with deionized water, acetone, methanol, and isopropyl alcohol using an ultrasonic bath for $10\ \mathrm{min}$ , sequentially. A compact $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $\left(\mathrm{cTiO}_{2}\right)$ layer was deposited on the FTO substrate via spray pyrolysis. To deposit the ${\mathrm{cTiO}}_{2}$ layer, $80~\mathrm{mL}$ of a titanium diisopropoxide bis(acetylacetonate)/ethanol $(1{:}10\ \mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v})$ solution was applied. After the spray pyrolysis, the $\mathrm{FTO/cTiO_{2}}$ substrate was annealed at $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ to improve the quality of film, after then slowly cooled to room temperature. $\mathrm{mpTiO}_{2}$ was deposited by spin-coating a diluted solution of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ paste. The ratio of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ paste to the ethanol/terpineol mixed solvent was 1:7. For the formamidinium lead iodide (FAPbI3) perovskite film, the perovskite solution was prepared with $1.5\mathrm{M}$ of FAPbI3 using FAI and $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ at a 4:1 ratio with a dimethylformamide and dimethyl sulfoxide mixing solvent. The precursor solution was spin-coated at $5{,}000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $50~\mathrm{s}.$ . During the coating, we dropped $1~\mathrm{mL}$ of diethyl ether onto the substrate $10\mathrm{~s~}$ after spinning for $10~\mathsf{s}.$ The spin-coated film was baked at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10\ \mathrm{min}$ . The hole-transport layer (HTL) was deposited by preparing $2{,}2{^{\prime}}{,}7{,}7{^{\prime}}$ -tetrakis[N,N-di(4-methoxyphenyl)amino]-9,9'- spirobifluorene (spiro-OMeTAD) in chlorobenzene $(90~\mathrm{mg/mL})$ ) and mixing it with $35.5\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of 4-tert-butylpyridine(tbp), $23\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of Li-bis(trifluoromethanesulfonyl)imide (Li-TFSI) $(520~\mathrm{mg/mL}$ acetonitrile) and $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of tris(2-(1h-pyrazol-1-yl)-4-tertbutylpyridine)-cobalt(III) tris(bis(trifluoromethylsulfonyl)imide) $\mathrm{\langle180~mg/mL}$ acetonitrile). The spiro-OMeTAD solution was spin-coated at $3,000\mathrm{rpm}$ for $30~\mathsf{s}.$ . All the processes were carried out in a dry atmosphere and no grabboxes were used. Finally, an Au counter electrode was deposited on the substrate using a thermal evaporation system.
## 2.3 Characterization
The morphology of the moth-eye pattern and samples was investigated using field-emission scanning electron microscopy (SEM) (Hitachi, S-4300) and atomic force microscopy (AFM) (probes). The optical properties were measured using a UV–vis spectrophotometer (Cary 5000) with an integrating sphere. The refractive index and absorption coefficient of the material were observed via spectroscopic ellipsometry (Woollam, M2000D). The crystallinity of the perovskite films was analyzed via X-ray diffraction (XRD) using a D8 Advance (Bruker) diffractometer equipped with $\operatorname{Cu}\operatorname{K}\alpha$ radiation $(\lambda=0.1542\mathrm{nm})$ . The solar cells were measured using a solar simulator (McScience, K3000 Lab solar cell $I{-}V$ measurement system, Class AAA) at $100\mathrm{\mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , with AM 1.5G illumination. External quantum efficiency (EQE) measurements were performed using a Quantx-300 QE system (Newport).
## 3 Result and discussion
To determine the optical properties of the flat FTO (F-FTO) and M-FTO, we measured the reflectance, transmittance, and diffused transmittance via ultraviolet–visible (UV–vis) spectroscopy with an integrating sphere in the wavelength range of $300{-}850~\mathrm{nm}$ . In the case of the diffused transmittance, both F-FTO and M-FTO had similar values (Fig. S2 in the ESM). However, the reflectance of M-FTO was approximately $4\%$ lower than that of F-FTO in all wavelength regions, and the transmittance of M-FTO was higher than that of F-FTO (Figs. 3(a) and 3(b)). The result of increasing the transmittance via the AR effect of the moth-eye pattern exhibited the same tendency observed in previous studies in various fields [31, 32].
Table 1 shows a figure of merit $\left(\varphi_{\mathrm{TC}}\right)$ that determines the appropriate degree of transmittance and electrical conduction for the transparent electrode. The figure of merit was calculated as follows [33, 34]
$$
\varphi_{\mathrm{TC}}=(T_{\mathrm{av}}/100)^{10}/R_{\mathrm{sh}}
$$
here, $T_{\mathrm{av}}$ represents the average transmittance from 380 to $780~\mathrm{nm}$ , and $R_{\mathrm{sh}}$ represents the sheet resistance. The sheet resistance of both FTOs was $7.09\Omega/\mathrm{sq}$ . The $T_{\mathrm{av}}$ of F-FTO was $78.97\%$ , and that of M-FTO was $82.44\%$ . The figure of merit for M-FTO was calculated to be 2.05, which was higher than that for F-FTO (1.33). This result indicates that the performance of the transparent electrode was increased through a simple process, i.e., direct printing.
Next, we calculated the effective refractive index of F-FTO and M-FTO using the following equation to confirm the reduction of the reflectance [23, 35].
$$
n_{\mathrm{eff}}=\sqrt{{n_{\mathrm{hsq}}}^{2}\times{f+{n_{\mathrm{air}}}^{2}}\times(1-f)}
$$
here, $n_{\mathrm{hsq}}$ and $n_{\mathrm{air}}$ $\stackrel{\cdot}{n}\approx1.0\stackrel{\cdot}{,}$ ) represent the refractive indices of HSQ and air, respectively, and $f$ represents the filling fraction of HSQ compared with air. The $n_{\mathrm{hsq}}$ in Fig. S3 in the ESM was measured using ellipsometry. Figure 2(e) and Table S1 in the ESM indicate that the HSQ filling fraction of the moth-eye pattern gradually increased in the downward direction. Figure 3(c) shows the calculated effective refractive index at $550~\mathrm{nm}$ for M-FTO and F-FTO in glass (soda-lime glass, $n\approx1.52;$ ), the HSQ residual layer $\mathbf{\zeta}_{n}\approx1.42)$ , the moth-eye pattern, and air $(n\approx1.0)$ ) according to Fig. 2(e) and Fig. S4 in the ESM. In the case of M-FTO, the effective refractive index gradually increased in the moth-eye pattern section, and there was no large difference in refractive index.
This result demonstrates the AR effect of the moth-eye pattern. In the case of F-FTO, there was a large reflection due to a large difference in the effective refractive index. Figure S5 in the ESM shows the distribution of the square of the electricfield intensity $(|E|^{2})$ at $550~\mathrm{nm}$ for the M-FTO and F-FTO. In the red and yellow areas, the electric field was strong. In the case of M-FTO, light scattering occurred in the moth-eye pattern section, yielding a red color, but in the air section, the total reflection was reduced, yielding a blue color. The result exhibits the same tendency as the reflectance measured via UV–vis spectroscopy (Fig. 3(a)) and the effective refractive index (Fig. 3(c)).
Figure 4(a) and Fig. S6 in the ESM show SEM images and schematics of PSCs with the optimized thickness. The top of the FTO substrate had a thickness of $600\mathrm{nm}$ , and it was coated with $\mathrm{cTiO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{\mpTiO}_{2}$ layers having thicknesses of 20 and $150\ \mathrm{nm}$ , respectively (as the ETLs). The active absorption layer of perovskite had a thickness of $700\ \mathrm{nm}$ FAPbI3, the spiro-OMeTAD had a thickness of $200\ \mathrm{nm}$ (as the HTL), and Au was deposited to $70\ \mathrm{nm}$ (as the counter electrode). Figure S7 in the ESM shows the XRD results for perovskite FAPbI3. The main FAPbI3 peak was sharp, and no secondary phase existed [36–38]. Figure 4(b) shows the absorption of the M-PSC and flat perovskite solar cell (F-PSC) with the aforementioned structure (Fig. 4(a)). The M-PSC had higher absorption than the F-PSC in the range of $300{-}900~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . The M-PSC and F-PSC had almost identical transmittance values, but the reflectance of the M-PSC was lower than that of the F-PSC, as shown in Fig. 4(b) (Fig. S8 in the ESM).
Figure 4(c) presents the current density–voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ curves for the photovoltaic performance of the F-PSC and M-PSC solar cells under $\mathrm{AM}\ 1.5\mathrm{G}$ illumination $(100\mathrm{\mW/cm^{2}})$ . As shown in Table 2, the F-PSC had an open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{OC}})$ of $1.06\mathrm{V},$ a $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of $23.70~\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , a fill factor (FF) of $79.09\%$ , and a PCE of $19.81\%$ . However, the M-PSC had a $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of $1.07\mathrm{V},$ a $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of $25.50\ \mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , a FF of $79.46\%$ , and a PCE of $21.77\%$ on an aperture area of $0.0804~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ . As shown in Fig. 4(d), the M-PSC exhibited an AR ability of the moth-eye pattern, according to the EQE spectra (Fig. 4(d)). To obtain accurate data, we calibrated using a standard reference detector equipped with
Quantx-300 QE system. These results exhibited the same tendency as the absorption graph (Fig. 4(b)), and it can be confirmed that the M-PSC had a higher value in the range of $300{-}800~\mathrm{{nm}}$ The integrated current densities from the EQE spectra were 25.18 and 23.97. This integrated current density almost coincided with the current density in the $J{-}V$ curve (Fig. 4(b)), with an error of $<3\%$ .
Figure 5 shows the statistical results of the cell performance (VOC, JSC, FF, and PCE) for four samples of the F-PSC and M-PSC. In the case of the $V_{\mathrm{OC}},$ there was little difference between the F-PSC and the M-PSC (Fig. 5(a)). This indicates that the perovskite materials were identically synthesized. Additionally, Fig. 5(c) shows that the FF values of the F-PSC and M-PSC were similar. The results for the $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ (Fig. 5(b)) and PCE (Fig. 5(d)) are notable. The average of $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of the M-PSC was $24.77~\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , which was approximately $3.1\%$ higher than that of the F-PSC $(24.02\mathrm{\mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ . This result is similar to the light-absorption increase due to the AR effect of the moth-eye pattern. Consequently, the PCE of the M-PSC increased by approximately $2\%$ compared with that of the F-PSC. Thus, the $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ and PCE were increased by the moth-eye pattern fabricated via the simple and inexpensive direct-printing method.
## 4 Conclusions
We fabricated FTO with a moth-eye pattern on the glass surface via direct printing, which is a next-generation patterning technology. When patterning the FTO surface, problems of gap filling may occur in the perovskite material coating. However, if the pattern is fabricated on the glass surface, the problem of gap filling for the coating does not occur, and the incident light is increased owing to the AR effect of the moth-eye pattern. In this study, we analyzed the effect of the moth-eye pattern on the glass surface and investigated the performance improvement of the perovskite. The light absorption of the M-PSC was increased by approximately $4\%$ , and the $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of the M-PSC was $25.50\ \mathrm{\mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , which was higher than that of the F-PSC $(23.70\ \mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ ). The PCE of the M-PSC was $21.77\%$ , which was $1.96\%$ higher than that of the F-PSC $(19.81\%)$ . In summary, we formed a moth-eye pattern on the glass surface via a simple and inexpensive direct-printing process, which increased the $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ and PCE.
